                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/equality+and+equivalence+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#ClassicalMoritaTheorem"} Classical Morita theorem
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definitions"} Definitions
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_algebra"} In algebra
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_homotopy_theory"} In homotopy theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_lie_groupoid_theory"} In Lie groupoid theory
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_tensor_category_theory"} In tensor category theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related Concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references_and_literature"} References and Literature
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the original sense, a
            %em Morita equivalence
            between two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            is two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodules"} bimodules
            between them that behave as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverses"} inverses
            to each other under
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+modules"} tensor product of modules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+a+2-category"} 2-categorical concept of equivalence
            for the two rings regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of rings, with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodules"} bimodules
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-morphisms"} 1-morphisms
            and bimodule homomorphisms (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intertwiners"}> intertwiners
            ) as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphisms"} 2-morphisms
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "bimodule#AsMorphismsInA2Category"} here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Watts+theorem"} Eilenberg-Watts theorem
            any such pair of bimodules equivalently induces a suitably “linear”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+modules"} categories of modules
            over the given rings.
          %p
            This is the original concept equivalence of rings due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kiiti+Morita"} Kiiti Morita
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#ClassicalMoritaTheorem"} below
          %p
            Nowadays, the term is applied in different but closely related senses in a wide range of mathematical fields, and one speaks of
            %em Morita equivalent
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theories"} algebraic theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theories"} geometric theories
            and so on.
          %p
            Typically, such Morita situations involve three ingredients: a ‘syntactic’ ground level to which the respective concept of
            %em Morita equivalence
            applies, a ‘hypersyntactic’ level obtained via
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+completion"} Cauchy completion
            %em small categories
            is defined as equivalence of their presheaf categories, with Cauchy completion as intermediate hypersyntactic level.
          %p
            So the broad intuition is that Morita equivalence is a
            %em coarse grained semantic equivalence that obtains between syntactic gadgets
            \- basically two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theories"} theories
            that have up to equivalence the same category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models"} models
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            ‘fgp-module’) bidirectional
            %em translation
            from one syntax into another.
          %p
            An important weakening of Morita equivalence is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+context"} Morita context
            (in older literature sometimes called pre-equivalence).
          %h2#ClassicalMoritaTheorem Classical Morita theorem
          %p
            Given (associative unital)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+unital+algebra"} algebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The categories of left
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
                and left
                = succeed "-modules" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            %li
              %p
                The categories of right
                = succeed "-modules" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                = succeed "-modules" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %li
              %p
                There are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodules"} bimodules
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}_R M_S
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}_S N_R
                such that the functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi R
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \otimes_R M
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi S
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \otimes_S N
                form an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+equivalence"} adjoint equivalence
                between the category of right
                = succeed "-modules" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                = succeed "-modules;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %li
              %p
                The algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                is isomorphic to the endomorphism algebra of a finitely generated projective
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generator"} generator
                in the category of left (or right)
                = succeed "-modules;" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %li
              %p
                The algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                is isomorphic to the endomorphism algebra of a finitely generated projective
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generator"} generator
                in the category of left (or right)
                = succeed "-modules." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            As usual, these results give results for rings when specialized to the case
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = \mathbb{Z}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            being a ring.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#in_algebra In algebra
          %p
            Two algebras over a commutative ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            are
            %strong Morita equivalent
            if the equivalent statements in the Morita theorem above are true. A
            %strong Morita equivalence
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+a+2-category"} equivalence
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Alg
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Alg}_k
            with
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+unital+algebras"} associative unital algebras
                over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                as objects,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodules"} bimodules
                as
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-morphisms"} 1-morphisms
            %li
              %p
                bimodule homomorphisms (“
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intertwiners"}> intertwiners
                ” as
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphisms"} 2-morphisms
          %p
            A
            %a{:href => "center+of+an+additive+category#examples"} theorem in ring theory
            says that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/center"} center
            of an algebra is isomorphic to the center of its category of modules and that Morita equivalent algebras have isomorphic centers. In particular, two
            %em commutative
            algebras are Morita equivalent precisely when they are isomorphic!
          %p
            This shows that the property of
            %em
              having center
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              up to isomorphism
            is stable within Morita equivalence classes. Properties of this kind are sufficiently important to deserve a special name:
          %p
            A property
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            of rings is called a
            %strong Morita invariant
            iff whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            holds for a ring
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            are Morita equivalent then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            also holds for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+ring"} simple
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+ring"} semisimple
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noetherian+ring"} Noetherian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artinian+ring"} Artinian
            (cf. Anderson and Fuller (1992)).
          %p
            The appearance of finitely generated projective modules in the
            %a{:href => "#ClassicalMoritaTheorem"} classical Morita theorem
            may seem unexpected, but it emerges from the following
          %p
          .num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R,S
              are algebras over a commutative ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is an
              = succeed "-bimodule." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo ,
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R,S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is a left adjoint in the bicategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alg_k
              of
              = succeed "-algebras," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is finitely generated projective as an
              = succeed "-module." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            There is a slick proof of this using
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %mi k
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = k Mod
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a one-object
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+completion"} Cauchy completion
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "-modules." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %h3#in_homotopy_theory In homotopy theory
          %p
            In any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            framework a
            %strong Morita equivalence
            between objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is a span
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mo ≃
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C \lt \stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow}
                  \\hat C
                  \\stackrel{\simeq}{\to} \gt
                  D
          %p where both legs are acyclic fibrations.
          %p
            In particular, if the ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical category
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
            %em factorization lemma
            (see there) ensures that
            %em every
            weak equivalence can be factored as a span of acyclic fibrations as above.
          %p
            Important fibrant objects are in particular
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"} infinity-groupoid
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
            and
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/omega-groupoid"} omega-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Golasinski"} Golasinski
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/folk+model+structure"} folk model structure
          %h3#in_lie_groupoid_theory In Lie groupoid theory
          %p
            A
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+morphism"} Morita morphism
              equivalence
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"} Lie groupoids
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor"} anafunctor
            that is invertible, equivalently an invertible
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilsum-Skandalis+morphism"} Hilsum-Skandalis morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bibundle"} bibundle
          %p
            Lie groupoids up to Morita equivalence are equivalent to
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stack"} differentiable stack
          %h3#in_tensor_category_theory In tensor category theory
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fusion+category"} fusion category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is Morita equivalent to another fusion category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            if there exist a
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            is equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reverse+category"} reverse category
            of
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
            to itself, namely
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo ∨
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi rev
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                      %mi Fun
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℳ
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi rev
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}\simeq (\mathcal{C}_{\mathcal{M}}^{\vee})^{rev}:=\mathsf{Fun}_{\mathcal{C}}(\mathcal{M},\mathcal{M})^{rev}.
          %p
            By the ‘reverse category’ of a monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            we mean the monoidal category with reversed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %msup
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi rev
                    %mi y
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\otimes^{rev}y:=y \otimes x
          %h2#related_concepts Related Concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+Morita+equivalence"} derived Morita equivalence
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+context"} Morita context
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Watts+theorem"} Eilenberg-Watts theorem
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generator"} generator
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} bimodule
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+module"} projective module
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Picard+group"} Picard group
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+completion"}> Cauchy completion
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karoubi+envelope"} Karoubi envelope
          %h2#references_and_literature References and Literature
          %p
            The concept is named after
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kiiti+Morita"} Kiiti Morita
          %p A beautiful classical exposition is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hyman+Bass"}> Hyman Bass
              , chapter II of
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algebraic K-theory
          %p The concept should be covered in any decent textbook on algebra and ring theory, e.g.:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                F. W. Anderson and K. R. Fuller,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Rings and Categories of Modules
            %li
              %p
                P. M. Cohn,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Further Algebra and Applications
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"}> Ross Street
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum Groups — A Path to Current Algebra
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511618505"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511618505
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://maths.mq.edu.au/~street/Quantum.pdf"} pdf
          %p For an early extension to domains other than ring theory see
          %ul
            %li
              H. Lindner,
              %em Morita equivalences of enriched categories
              , Cah. Top. Géom. Diff. Cat
              %strong 15
              no.4 (1974) pp.377-397. (
              %a{:href => "http://archive.numdam.org/article/CTGDC_1974__15_4_377_0.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p The generalizations to graded rings, Hopf algebras and corings are studied in references
          %ul
            %li
              A. Marcus,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Equivalences induced by graded bimodules
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homology of fully graded algebras, Morita and derived equivalences
              = succeed ":1\u20132" do
                %strong 133
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0022-4049(97)00196-5"} doi
            %li
              S. Caenepeel, J.Vercruysse, Shuanhong Wang,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Morita theory for corings and cleft entwining structures
              = succeed "1" do
                %strong 276
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jalgebra.2004.02.002"} doi
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefaan+Caenepeel"}> Stefaan Caenepeel
              , Septimiu Crivei, Andrei Marcus,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitsuhiro+Takeuchi"} Mitsuhiro Takeuchi
              = succeed "," do
                %em Morita equivalences induced by bimodules over Hopf–Galois extensions
              %strong 314
              (2007) 267–302
              %a{:href => "https://core.ac.uk/download/pdf/81180359.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriella+B%C3%B6hm"}> Gabriella Böhm
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joost+Vercruysse"} Joost Vercruysse
              = succeed "," do
                %em Morita theory for comodules over corings
              = succeed ":9" do
                %strong 37
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1080/00927870902747993"} doi
          %p The case of algebraic theories is covered in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> F. Borceux
                ,
                %em Handbook of Categorical Algebra 2
                , CUP 1994. (sec. 3.12)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiri+Adamek"}> J. Adámek
                , M. Sobral, L. Sousa,
                %em Morita equivalence of many-sorted algebraic theories
                , JA
                %strong 297
                (2006) pp.361-371. (
                %a{:href => "https://estudogeral.sib.uc.pt/bitstream/10316/4616/1/filee8df8c9585d34e1a8d56fdaf0460d008.pdf"}> preprint
                )
          %p For the use in O. Caramello’s ‘toposes as bridges’- approach that brings out the logical side of the concept:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Olivia+Caramello"}> O. Caramello
              ,
              %em Topos-theoretic background
              , ms. 2014. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.oliviacaramello.com/Unification/ToposTheoreticPreliminariesOliviaCaramello.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p Other references include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ralf+Meyer"}> Ralf Meyer
                ,
                %em Morita equivalence in algebra and geometry
                \. (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/MeyerMoritaEquivalence-2.pdf", :title => "PDF"}> PDF
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/I.+Dell%27Ambrogio"}> I. Dell'Ambrogio
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goncalo+Tabuada"} Goncalo Tabuada
                %em A Quillen model structure for classical Morita theory and a tensor categorification of the Brauer group
                , arXiv:1211.2309 (2012). (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/pdf/1211.2309v1"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans+Porst"}> Hans Porst
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Generalized Morita Theories: The power of categorical algebra
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.uni-bremen.de/~porst/dvis/SAMSnoticesCorr.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"} Francis Borceux
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Enrico+Vitale"} Enrico Vitale
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the notion of bimodel for functorial semantics
                = succeed ":283\u2013295" do
                  %strong 2
                %a{:href => "http://perso.uclouvain.be/enrico.vitale/BimodAPCS.pdf"} pdf
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morita_equivalence"} Morita equivalence
